From 5a3695219acfb2204e562fdd917c2b6be9d47752 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: juli <woojoohyeon@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 08:25:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Redesign around short-term trend hypothesis; fix Colab OOM
 and eval bias

Season-diverse holdout showed the YoY seasonal features (0917) have no
season-general effect, so the pipeline now tests the hypothesis that recent
momentum, newness and age-group trends drive fast-fashion purchases instead.

- features.py: 18 opt-in short-term trend features; YoY features now opt-in;
  labels, typing and output done inside duckdb (COPY to parquet) so no huge
  pandas frame is built. Defaults still produce the 0906 35-column set.
- candidates.py: optional age-bucket trend candidate source; pool assembly via
  long-format arrays + duckdb GROUP BY (~520 -> ~130 bytes/pair, identical
  output); deterministic ALS item indexing and tie-breaking.
- train_protocol.py / run_trend_experiment.py / 09 notebook: train on
  train2-4, early-stop on train1, report on val/holdout/season weeks only,
  scored the way the submission is built; base/trend_feat/trend_full ablation
  with paired customer bootstrap; refit + predict with age-trend cold start.
  Runs the same on local and Google Colab (High-RAM).
- multi_week_eval.py: auto-enables YoY/trend columns needed by scored models.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DQARBgq55zxiVNGU2ijZVQ
